        key: "*** TWO ROUNDS PREDICTED THIS WOULD BE A SOLVER RATHER THAN A DEVICE, AND THE MEASUREMENT OVERTURNED IT. *** The prediction was right about the GATE -- exact interpolation, sum(w)=1, optimality by a thousand random perturbations, the reported variance agreeing with varianceOfWeights: all self-consistency, nothing compared to an answer computed another way. It was wrong about the METHOD, because gamma is a CALLER-SUPPLIED FUNCTION and two choices have answers known in advance. WITH gamma(h)=h IN ONE DIMENSION the field is Brownian and therefore MARKOV, so the two bracketing samples screen every other one and ordinary kriging collapses to plain LINEAR INTERPOLATION: matched to 2.7e-15 over six irregularly spaced samples, with four of six weights driven to machine zero at every query -- a structural claim the module's gate never makes, since it checks that weights SUM to one and never WHICH samples carry them. The variance is the Brownian bridge (x0-a)(b-x0)/(b-a) at a ratio of exactly 2.000000000000 with a spread of 8.9e-16 across five queries, and THE 2 IS THE SEMIVARIOGRAM CONVENTION ANNOUNCING ITSELF rather than an error -- a mistake would drift across the sweep and this does not. WITH A PURE NUGGET there is no spatial information at all, so the only unbiased answer left is the arithmetic mean: value 1.625 against 1.625 with a difference of EXACTLY ZERO, weights 1/n to 5.6e-17, variance exactly 1+1/n. THE LOAD-BEARING NEGATIVE FAILS BY A PREDICTED AMOUNT: the screen is a property of exponent 1 exactly, and gamma(h)=h^p opens it LINEARLY in the departure -- 6.04e-3, 3.02e-2, 6.10e-2 at p = 1.05, 1.25, 1.5, which is 0.1212 x (p-1) with a 1.2% spread over a tenfold range. Nothing was tuned; one function is evaluated at four exponents.",
