        key: "THIS SOLVER HAD NO GATE AT ALL, and it describes itself as a correct 2D FLIP/PIC fluid solver. It was invisible inside a count of 125 ungated modules, 116 of which are demo scenes importing no physics; correcting that denominator turned an unactionable number into a list of nine, and this was the first name on it. TWO EXACT KEYS AND ONE HONEST INEXACT ONE. Free fall: before the block lands every particle falls at exactly g, measured against g*t to 1.4e-7 worst -- f32 epsilon territory, not a chosen tolerance -- and it tests the INTEGRATOR alone, since 2 and 80 pressure iterations give the same falling speed. Conservation: 1344 particles in and 1344 out across 200 steps including the landing and splash, an exact integer, with none escaping the domain. Projection: after landing, more pressure iterations drive divergence down 238x (3.14e+0 at 2 iterations to 1.32e-2 at 80) -- but NOT MONOTONICALLY, since 5 iterations measures worse than 2, and a free-surface fluid mid-splash is not a well-posed convergence study. THE OBVIOUS TEST OF THE PROJECTION IS VACUOUS: at 25 steps the block is still in free fall at meanSpeed 4.0875 = g*0.4167 exactly, divergence is 9e-7 whatever the iteration count, and the check passes while testing nothing -- which is why the real checks run to 160. AND THE SAME TRAP CAUGHT THE NEXT CHECK ON THE NEXT LINE: pure PIC and pure FLIP read an identical 19.6200 at 120 steps because that is g times two seconds and the block has not landed. Past the landing they separate, PIC 1.5110 against FLIP 3.2418, with FLIP the faster because it carries the grid CHANGE back and keeps energy -- the direction theory requires, not merely a difference.",
